{ <sh_left>/<sh_right> with two literals are folded here, so the constant that ends up in the
  script has to be the one Kontakt would have computed itself.

  Kontakt evaluates both as the native 32 bit shift of its host, which takes only the low five
  bits of the count. The expectations below are what Kontakt 7 printed for the same expressions
  with the operands held in variables, where neither compiler can fold them away:

    sh_right(-1, 31) = -1            sh_left(1, 32) = 1
    sh_right(-1, 32) = -1            sh_left(1, 33) = 2
    sh_right(-1, 33) = -1            sh_left(1, 31) = -2147483648
    sh_right(1, 32)  = 1             sh_left(-1, 1) = -2
    sh_right(8, 2)   = 2
    sh_right(1, -1)  = 0             sh_left(1, -1) = -2147483648

  A count of 32 or more and a negative count are the interesting ones: they are undefined
  behaviour on the int32_t the folder uses, so getting them right is not something the host
  compiler can be trusted with. <sh_right(-1, 32)> is not academic - it is how the bit list
  helpers in sonu-libraries spell a full 32 bit mask. }

{ a right shift keeps the sign, and a count at or past the width wraps to its low five bits }
// EXPECT message\("sr31 " & -1\)
// EXPECT message\("sr32 " & -1\)
// EXPECT message\("sr33 " & -1\)
// EXPECT message\("sr1x32 " & 1\)
// EXPECT message\("sr8x2 " & 2\)
{ a left shift runs on the bit pattern, so reaching the sign bit gives the negative value }
// EXPECT message\("sl32 " & 1\)
// EXPECT message\("sl33 " & 2\)
// EXPECT message\("sl31 " & -2147483648\)
// EXPECT message\("slneg " & -2\)
{ a negative count masks to 31 }
// EXPECT message\("srm1 " & 0\)
// EXPECT message\("slm1 " & -2147483648\)
{ nothing is left for the engine to evaluate }
// EXPECT_NOT sh_right\(
// EXPECT_NOT sh_left\(

on init
    message("sr31 " & sh_right(-1, 31))
    message("sr32 " & sh_right(-1, 32))
    message("sr33 " & sh_right(-1, 33))
    message("sr1x32 " & sh_right(1, 32))
    message("sr8x2 " & sh_right(8, 2))
    message("sl32 " & sh_left(1, 32))
    message("sl33 " & sh_left(1, 33))
    message("sl31 " & sh_left(1, 31))
    message("slneg " & sh_left(-1, 1))
    message("srm1 " & sh_right(1, -1))
    message("slm1 " & sh_left(1, -1))
end on
